---
import Context from '../../layouts/Context.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
import Thumb from '../../components/Thumb.astro';
import Lightbox from '../../components/Lightbox.astro';
const g = (id: string) => url(`glossary/#${id}`);
const sources = [
  { cite: 'Adapted from Thomas Hemphill, "Textual Criticism of the NT: background, theory and principles", slides and recorded talk: the UBS5 ratings of certainty, when a translator needs to make a textual decision, footnoting significant variation, and John 1:19. Used with permission.', checked: true },
  { cite: 'Adapted from Thomas Hemphill, "Textual Criticism of the NT: practical examples", recorded talk: Romans 16:7 and what the {A} rating covers. Used with permission.', checked: true },
  { cite: 'The Greek New Testament, 5th rev. ed. (United Bible Societies / Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft, 2014), introduction pp. 8–9 on the ratings, as quoted in the Hemphill slides above.', checked: true },
  { cite: 'Image: 𝔓⁶⁶, John 1:14–21, via CNTR. Public domain in the United States (Bridgeman v. Corel, 1999).', checked: true },
  { cite: 'Bruce M. Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., on Romans 16:7 (read aloud in the Hemphill practical talk above; the book itself not checked).', checked: false },
];
---
<Context title="Which variants deserve your attention" label="Guide" lastChecked="2026-11-20" sources={sources}
  description="Not all variants weigh the same. How to sort them by meaning and by the UBS rating, and when to add a footnote.">
  <p class="lead">Greek manuscripts differ in many thousands of places. Most of these differences leave the meaning of the verse alone. Only a few need a translator to stop and study.</p>

  <h2>Kinds of variants</h2>
  <p>A <em>variant</em> is a place where manuscripts do not have the same wording. Some barely matter; some change what a verse says. Here they are from lightest to heaviest.</p>

  <figure class="scale">
    <div class="scale-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span>lighter</span><span>heavier</span></div>
    <ol class="kinds">
      <li class="k1">
        <h3>Spelling</h3>
        <p>The same word written another way, often a copying slip. The UBS apparatus leaves most of these out, because they do not affect translation.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="k2">
        <h3>Small words</h3>
        <p>A pronoun or short phrase that some manuscripts have and others lack. In John 1:19 some put <span lang="grc">πρὸς αὐτόν</span> ("to him") after "sent", some put it later in the sentence, and some leave it out. The meaning stays the same.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="k3">
        <h3>Changes of meaning</h3>
        <p>The wording changes what the verse says. In Romans 16:7 the letters <span lang="grc">ΙΟΥΝΙΑΝ</span> can take different accents and become a woman's name (Junia) or a man's name (Junias). That decides whether a woman is named among the apostles.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="k4">
        <h3>Long passages</h3>
        <p>Whole verses or stories present in some manuscripts and missing in others. UBS prints John 7:53–8:11 inside <a href={g('double-brackets')}>⟦ ⟧</a>: the editors think it was added later, but it is very old and important.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </figure>

  <h2>Sorting a variant</h2>
  <p>Ask two separate questions about each one.</p>

  <div class="two-q">
    <section class="q">
      <p class="q-num">1</p>
      <h3>Does it change the meaning in your language?</h3>
      <p>Test it in your own language, not in Greek. In John 1:19, "to him" adds nothing in English: if the leaders sent people to ask John a question, they sent them to John. Most English versions (NIV, ESV, NLT) leave it out; a few (NASB, NJB) keep it. An English translator need not study it further. A language that likes to repeat such words may decide differently.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="q">
      <p class="q-num">2</p>
      <h3>How sure were the UBS editors?</h3>
      <p>The UBS text gives each variant in its apparatus a <a href={g('rating')}>rating</a>, shown below.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <figure class="ms">
    <Thumb file="img/ms/m10066-001v.webp" width={900} height={1017} alt="A papyrus page of Greek capital letters with no spaces between words, containing John 1:14–21." caption="𝔓⁶⁶, John 1:14–21. Public domain in the United States (Bridgeman v. Corel, 1999), via CNTR." />
    <figcaption>𝔓⁶⁶, a papyrus with John 1:19 on this page. Public domain in the United States (Bridgeman v. Corel, 1999); image via CNTR.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="ratings" role="list">
    <div class="r" role="listitem"><span class="sig">{'{A}'}</span><span class="meter" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:100%"></i></span><p>The text is certain.</p></div>
    <div class="r" role="listitem"><span class="sig">{'{B}'}</span><span class="meter" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:75%"></i></span><p>The text is almost certain.</p></div>
    <div class="r low" role="listitem"><span class="sig">{'{C}'}</span><span class="meter" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:50%"></i></span><p>The committee had difficulty deciding which reading to put in the text.</p></div>
    <div class="r low" role="listitem"><span class="sig">{'{D}'}</span><span class="meter" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:25%"></i></span><p>The committee had great difficulty deciding. This is rare.</p></div>
    <div class="r low" role="listitem"><span class="sig"><a href={g('diamond')}>♦</a></span><span class="meter" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:0%"></i></span><p>No decision was possible. Only in the Catholic letters, in UBS5.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">Meanings as the UBS editors give them. The bars show only the order.</p>
  <p>Words in square brackets in the UBS Greek text usually have a {'{C}'} rating or lower. <span lang="grc">πρὸς αὐτόν</span> in John 1:19 is one example.</p>

  <h2>What a rating does not tell you</h2>
  <aside class="callout">
    <p>A rating answers only the question the committee asked. Romans 16:7 is rated {'{A}'}, but Metzger's <i>Textual Commentary</i> says that covers only the spelling of the name, not the accent. The oldest manuscripts had no accents. So the letters are certain, but the {'{A}'} does not settle "man or woman?". Only the commentary tells you this.</p>
  </aside>
  <p>The commentary also notes that the woman's name Junia is common in inscriptions from Rome, while the man's name Junias has not been found there. Other scholars point to some evidence for the man's name, so the question is still discussed.</p>
  <p>The ratings are a committee's opinion, and scholars have criticised them. See <a href={url('guide/apparatus/#ratings')}>what the ratings do and do not tell you</a>.</p>

  <h2>When you need to decide</h2>
  <p>The UBS text is an eclectic text: at each place the editors chose the best reading from many manuscripts. So most of the time you can simply follow it. Make your own textual decision only when:</p>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>a Bible in a language of wider communication (LWC) that your readers know differs from the UBS text;</li>
    <li>the variant is rated {'{C}'} or lower and changes the meaning; or</li>
    <li>the variant is part of a larger problem of interpreting the verse.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The UBS apparatus does not always list the reading of the Textus Receptus (the Greek text behind the King James Version and many older Bibles). If your readers use a Bible based on it, compare that Bible directly.</p>
  <blockquote class="policy">
    <p class="small muted">Some teams write a policy like this into their project brief:</p>
    <p>"We follow the UBS text, unless it has a {'{C}'} rating or lower; then we consider the base text of the LWC Bible."</p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>When to add a footnote</h2>
  <p>A footnote can show readers a significant variant: one that changes the meaning, or one where your text differs from a Bible they know. Spelling and small words that leave the meaning alone do not need one. Whether you use footnotes at all is up to your project's policy. Some translations, oral ones for example, cannot use them.</p>

  <h2>Decision guide</h2>
  <p>Work down the steps. Stop as soon as one tells you to.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Does it change the meaning in your language?</strong> <span class="out">No → follow the UBS text.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Does a Bible your readers use follow a different reading?</strong> <span class="out warn">Yes → study the variant.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Is it rated {'{C}'}, {'{D}'} or ♦?</strong> <span class="out warn">Yes → study the variant.</span> <span class="out">{'{A}'} or {'{B}'} → follow the UBS text, but check what the rating covers (as in Romans 16:7).</span></li>
    <li><strong>Study it.</strong> Read the Translator's Notes and the Translator's Handbook, look at the apparatus, read the commentaries if needed, and compare other translations, especially ones your readers use.</li>
    <li><strong>Decide.</strong> If the variant is significant, consider a footnote.</li>
  </ol>

  <section class="unchecked-box" aria-labelledby="unverified">
    <h2 id="unverified">Not yet verified <span class="tag">not yet checked</span></h2>
    <p class="small muted">Not checked against a source in our files yet.</p>
    <p>Word order is often named as one of the commonest kinds of variant. Greek word order is flexible, so most of these do not change the meaning.</p>
  </section>
  <Lightbox />
</Context>
<style>
  .scale { margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  .scale-bar { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font: 0.75rem var(--sans); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--ink-2);
    padding-bottom: .35rem; border-bottom: 4px solid var(--rubric); }
  .kinds { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: .75rem 0 0; display: grid; gap: .75rem; }
  @media (min-width: 640px) { .kinds { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .kinds li { background: var(--paper-2); border-radius: 8px; padding: .75rem 1rem; border-left: 4px solid var(--rule); }
  .kinds h3 { margin: 0 0 .25rem; font: 600 1rem var(--sans); }
  .kinds p { margin: 0; }
  .kinds .k2 { border-left-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--rubric) 35%, var(--rule)); }
  .kinds .k3 { border-left-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--rubric) 70%, var(--rule)); }
  .kinds .k4 { border-left-color: var(--rubric); }
  .two-q { display: grid; gap: .75rem; }
  @media (min-width: 640px) { .two-q { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .q { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 8px; padding: .75rem 1rem; }
  .q h3 { margin: 0 0 .4rem; font: 600 1rem var(--sans); }
  .q-num { margin: 0; font: 700 1.6rem var(--serif); color: var(--rubric); line-height: 1; }
  .ms { display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: center; margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  .ms figcaption { font: .85rem var(--sans); color: var(--ink-2); }
  .ratings { display: grid; gap: .4rem; margin: 1rem 0 .25rem; }
  .r { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2.5rem 3rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: .6rem; align-items: center; padding: .4rem .6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 4px; }
  .r p { margin: 0; }
  .sig { font-family: var(--sigla); font-weight: 700; }
  .meter { height: 6px; background: var(--paper-2); border-radius: 3px; overflow: hidden; }
  .meter i { display: block; height: 100%; background: var(--ink-2); }
  .r.low { background: var(--warn-bg); color: var(--warn-ink); }
  .callout { border-left: 4px solid var(--rubric); background: var(--paper-2); padding: .5rem 1rem; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .policy { margin: 1rem 0; padding: .5rem 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 8px; font-family: var(--serif); }
  .policy p:last-child { font-style: italic; }
  .steps { list-style: none; padding: 0; counter-reset: s; }
  .steps li { counter-increment: s; position: relative; padding: 0 0 1rem 1.6rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--rule); margin-left: .9rem; }
  .steps li:last-child { border-left-color: transparent; }
  .steps li::before { content: counter(s); position: absolute; left: -.95rem; top: 0; width: 1.8rem; height: 1.8rem; border-radius: 50%;
    display: grid; place-items: center; background: var(--rubric); color: var(--paper); font: 700 .9rem var(--sans); }
  .out { display: inline-block; margin-top: .25rem; font: .9rem var(--sans); padding: .1rem .5rem; border-radius: 4px; background: var(--paper-2); }
  .out.warn { background: var(--warn-bg); color: var(--warn-ink); }
  .unchecked-box { border: 1px dashed var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: 0 1rem 0.25rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .unchecked-box h2 { margin-top: 1rem; }
</style>
